---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import db from '../data/power_stations.json';

const count = db.power_stations.length;
const title = 'About WattLibrary — Sources & Runtime Methodology';
const description = 'How WattLibrary verifies portable power station specifications, calculates AC and DC runtimes, and stays 100% independent.';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@graph': [
    {
      '@type': 'AboutPage',
      name: title,
      description,
      url: new URL(Astro.url.pathname, Astro.site).href,
    },
    {
      '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
      itemListElement: [
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: new URL('/', Astro.site).href },
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'About' },
      ],
    },
  ],
};
---
<Base {title} {description} {schema}>
  <nav class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> › About</nav>
  <h1>About WattLibrary</h1>
  <p style="max-width:680px;">
    This site started with a spreadsheet. Comparing power stations meant keeping fifteen browser tabs open, because every brand
    reports its numbers differently: one quotes surge wattage in the headline, another buries it in a PDF manual, a third
    invents its own name for voltage-dropping boost mode. The spreadsheet kept growing until it made more sense to publish it.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the numbers come from</h2>
  <p style="max-width:680px;">
    Manufacturer documentation, first and foremost: US product pages, technical spec sheets, and PDF user manuals. Every listing
    in our {count}-model database is checked against official specs (last full verification: {db.updated}) and links directly to its
    manufacturer source. Where regional variants differ (for example US 120V AC outlet counts vs EU 230V versions), we standardize
    on US 120V specifications.
  </p>

  <h2>How runtime &amp; solar estimates work</h2>
  <p style="max-width:680px;">
    Every model page, comparison table, guide, and calculator on WattLibrary uses one shared math model — rounded <strong>down</strong>
    so estimates stay honest rather than flattering:
  </p>
  <ul style="max-width:680px;">
    <li><strong>AC loads:</strong> <code>capacity_wh × 0.85 ÷ average_watts</code>. The 85% factor accounts for typical pure sine wave inverter conversion losses.</li>
    <li><strong>12V DC / USB-C loads (CPAP DC, Starlink Mini DC):</strong> <code>capacity_wh × 0.95 ÷ average_watts</code>, since bypassing the AC inverter avoids most conversion loss.</li>
    <li><strong>Compressor appliances (refrigerators &amp; freezers):</strong> Evaluated on two separate tests — a startup surge check against the station's surge rating (e.g., 900W for a full-size fridge) plus a 24-hour duty-cycled average draw (60W for a full-size fridge, consistent with EnergyGuide kWh/year labels). If a station cannot start the compressor, it gets no runtime credit regardless of battery size.</li>
    <li><strong>Solar recharge hours:</strong> <code>capacity_wh ÷ (min(panel_watts, max_solar_input) × 0.78)</code>, accounting for real-world panel harvest and MPPT losses rather than idealized lab numbers.</li>
  </ul>
  <p style="max-width:680px;">
    You can plug your own wattages and solar panel ratings into the <a href="/calculator/">Runtime &amp; Solar MPPT Calculator</a>, or browse the
    <a href="/guides/">worked appliance guides</a> to see the numbers across every station.
  </p>

  <h2>Independence</h2>
  <p style="max-width:680px;">
    No ads, no affiliate links, no sponsored placements. No brand pays to be listed and no brand can pay to change its numbers.
    If that ever changes, the disclosure will be on every affected page, not buried here.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p style="max-width:680px;">
    Spotted a wrong number or a spec revision? Email us at <a href="mailto:hello@wattlibrary.com">hello@wattlibrary.com</a>.
    Verified corrections ship within 24 hours, and we're genuinely grateful for them — a database is only worth its accuracy.
  </p>
</Base>
